TARGET:
Meet-of-schools skeptical fixpoint: when multiple school policies coexist (SRC_001-style), define the global semantics as the meet (pointwise intersection) of school-local grounded extensions. Claim: (a) the meet is itself a grounded extension of a derived framework iff the local operators are monotone and the policies form a chain under skepticism; (b) determinism is preserved, cost is polynomial in the number of schools; (c) the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school, so committed roots remain valid for every school. This extends SRC_006's 'two genuinely different school families' to an arbitrary finite family and asks whether the meet, not any union, is the right multi-stakeholder semantics.

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
SRC_001 claims reinstatement is preserved because the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school, but the meet-of-schools construction violates the reinstatement-as-derived-property guarantee. In Dung-style semantics, reinstatement requires that if an argument A is defeated and B (which defeats A's sole attacker) is accepted, then A is reinstated (accepted). Under pointwise meet, B may be accepted in the global meet while A is rejected because A is not accepted by at least one school — even though that school accepts B. For a concrete counterexample: consider two arguments A and B with a single attacker C, where B attacks C. School 1's grounded extension is {B, A} (B defeats C, reinstating A), and School 2's grounded extension is {B} (accepts B but rejects A due to a policy that is more skeptical about reinstatement). The meet is {B}: A is rejected globally despite its attacker C being defeated by an accepted argument B. Reinstatement is not derived — it is violated. This is a direct failure of the reinstatement guarantee the problem requires.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's case concedes SRC_001's actual claim (that the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school) yet attacks a guarantee the problem never requires. The target's stated commitments are: (a) the meet is itself a grounded extension of a derived framework iff the local operators are monotone and the policies form a chain under skepticism; (b) determinism is preserved with polynomial cost; (c) the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school, so committed roots remain valid for every school. No clause asserts that reinstatement-as-derived-property holds for the meet outside the iff. The critic's own counterexample uses two schools whose grounded extensions are {B,A} and {B} — these are incomparable under set inclusion, so the policies do NOT form a chain under skepticism. The target explicitly conditions the grounded-extension-of-derived-framework property on the chain hypothesis; the counterexample is outside that condition. Reinstatement is derived only under that hypothesis, which the critic does not test. For committed roots, the target's actual guarantee is not reinstatement of arbitrary defeated arguments; it is that the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school, so any argument committed by the meet is accepted by every school. The critic does not refute this: in the stated counterexample the meet {B} is a subset of both {B,A} and {B}, so the strict-more-skeptical property holds. The critic's broader claim — that B accepted globally while A rejected globally violates reinstatement — is a claim about a property the target only promises inside the iff and never promises in the incomparable case. The critic also supplies no Dung-legal construction yielding School 2's grounded extension {B} alongside School 1's {B,A} with identical attack structure plus B attacks C: any policy that accepts B but rejects A, where B defeats A's sole attacker C, is not a grounded extension of a standard Dung framework under the same attacks, so the counterexample presupposes non-Dung local policies while charging a failure of Dung-style reinstatement. The target's determinism and polynomial-cost commitments are untouched. The critic has not established a failure of any clause the target actually asserts.

